when over the last 40 years since the  time we started our Galapagos research  there have been two or three major  changes in general understanding of how  evolution works the first is a big  development in computer technology which  is allow people to analyze large  quantities of data the second one is a  very large development in molecular  genetics which has given many biologists  the tools to examining evolution at  genetic level and the third one is the  fuel studies that have been done that  allow people to understand just how  rapidly evolution takes place not over  centuries not over decades but over a  short period of time such as a year for  organisms as large as birds the general  theory of evolution has changed very  little what actually has changed is the  techniques that have allowed us to study  it and one of the biggest ones is the  genetic technique so now that we are  able to sequence whole genomes we've  been able to look at the genes and that  are responsible for some of the changes  that we have seen in the field so this  has been extremely exciting and so it is  more that we are able to go in depth to  a much greater degree than they were  before
 

